# versions-spoa.env — pinned versions for the bundled SPOA hub image.
#
# This file is sourced by CI to download the hub binary + plugin .so files
# from each upstream project's GitLab Generic Package Registry. The
# `docs/site/docs/operations/spoa-hub.md` page is auto-generated
# from this file and CI fails if the rendered output drifts.
#
# Each version string is the upstream git tag (with leading `v`) and
# matches the path segment inside that project's Generic Package Registry
# at `packages/generic/<pkg>/<tag>/`.

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub:spoa-hub registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=spoa-hub
# v0.9.0 processes pipelined NOTIFY frames concurrently instead of
# one-at-a-time per connection (the serial loop collapsed effective
# concurrency to the connection count and was the source of the ~5ms
# SPOA overhead), and bounds total in-flight frames to the usable CPU
# core count with a process-wide backpressure gate: when the gate is
# full the connection read loops stop reading so HAProxy is
# backpressured (the excess queues in HAProxy's buffers) instead of the
# hub flooding its per-plugin admission queues and shedding 503s under
# saturation on a capacity-limited box. Homelab-verified 0% 503 across
# c=16→96 heavy-POST WAF saturation. New metrics: spoa_inflight_frames,
# spoa_inflight_gate_capacity, spoa_backpressure_waits_total. Also adds
# opt-in adaptive per-plugin concurrency (ADR-0002; a Gradient2
# latency-feedback controller resizes the admission semaphore at
# runtime from per-call service time) and cuts per-frame dispatch
# allocation (the request is copied once at the FFI boundary and shared
# by refcount instead of deep-copied per plugin group; metric handles
# are resolved once at registry build) — ~15% less dispatch overhead on
# WAF-sized request bodies. No plugin-API change (stays 0.7.1 / vtable
# v3). Via haproxy-spoa-hub !209 + !210 + !211 + v0.9.0 release.
#
# v0.8.0 bounds plugin execution under overload: per-plugin
# max_concurrency / max_queue / queue_timeout_ms admission controls
# plus a derived-or-explicit max_blocking_threads process cap. Excess
# work returns a host-owned `<plugin>.__status=overloaded` variable
# instead of entering Tokio's unbounded blocking queue; timeout,
# error, and dependency-driven `skipped` outcomes are explicit, and
# timed-out blocking calls retain their admission permit until the
# native call actually finishes. The chart's generated hub TOML
# emits these keys, so HAPTIC requires >= v0.8.0. Ships plugin-api
# 0.7.1 (define_plugin instance state via `self`; source-only, no
# vtable ABI change). Via haproxy-spoa-hub !191 + !193 (release).
#
# v0.7.4 prevents SIGSEGV / exit 139 when a config reload removes
# the final instance of a plugin such as Coraza. The hub still
# drains, shuts down, and destroys every retired instance, but
# retains native plugin-library mappings until process exit so
# foreign runtimes, TLS destructors, plugin statics, and background
# callbacks can never execute against dlclose'd code. Config and
# instance state remain hot-reloadable; replacing a plugin .so
# requires a hub restart, which a HAPTIC image upgrade provides by
# rolling the HAProxy pods. Fixed via haproxy-spoa-hub !187 (fix) +
# !188 (release).
#
# v0.7.3 closes the reload-loss window the v0.7.0 drain phase
# still left open (haptic race B, haptic issue #47): the reload
# sequence is now `swap → quiesce → drain → shutdown`
# (registry::swap_and_retire). After the atomic registry swap
# routes new SPOP frames to the new plugin set, the hub waits for
# every NOTIFY dispatch still holding the pre-swap registry
# snapshot to finish BEFORE draining background work and shutting
# the old set down. Previously drain_all ran immediately after the
# swap — dispatches that had loaded the pre-swap snapshot had not
# yet enqueued their fire-and-forget follow-up (the mirror
# plugin's runtime.spawn'd HTTP request), drain observed nothing
# in flight, and the work died with the old plugin generation
# (observed as ~50% mirror-rate flakiness on gateway-conformance
# shards, depending on which side of a reload each NOTIFY burst
# landed). Also makes unhandled NOTIFYs loud: a message no plugin
# in the CURRENT config is registered for now logs WARN and bumps
# the new spoa_messages_unhandled_total{message} counter instead
# of vanishing with a DEBUG log and an empty ACK. SPOP has no
# message-level error frame, so loud-unhandled is the ceiling —
# such messages are still not SERVED; a mirror-fanout shrink
# mid-flight loses those mirrors with a warning (mirrors are
# best-effort by design). New
# spoa_hub_config_reload_quiesce_forced_total counter flags
# reloads whose in-flight dispatches outlived the budget; quiesce
# and drain share the existing reload_drain_timeout_ms budget
# (0 still opts out of both). No plugin-API change (stays 0.7.0 /
# vtable v3). Fixed via haproxy-spoa-hub !180 (fix) + !181
# (release).
#
# v0.7.0 closed the architectural "reload must drain in-flight
# requests before swapping" concern that v0.6.2 documented as a
# deferred follow-up (haproxy-spoa-hub#3). The reload sequence
# became `swap → drain → shutdown` rather than `swap → shutdown`:
# plugins with fire-and-forget background work get
# `drain(timeout_ms) -> bool` called between the registry swap and
# `shutdown_all`, blocking until their in-flight tasks complete or
# the deadline elapses. Plugin-API bumped from v2 to v3 to carry
# the new vtable field; append-only ABI discipline means v1/v2
# plugins keep loading on v0.7.x hubs (skip drain — correct for
# the synchronous-only majority: coraza, external-auth, sso-auth,
# fingerprinting, maxmind), and v3 plugins keep loading on v0.6.x
# hubs (those hubs ignore the appended field — same behaviour as
# today's hub for those plugins). The mirror and otel plugins
# bumped below are the v3-consumer releases that actually override
# drain. Two new metrics surface drain quiescence:
# `spoa_hub_config_reload_drain_duration_seconds` (histogram) and
# `spoa_hub_config_reload_drain_forced_total{plugin}` (counter).
# Operator-tunable via the optional `[hub] reload_drain_timeout_ms`
# config field; default derives from
# `max(plugin.timeout_ms) * 1.5`, clamped to [1000ms, 30_000ms].
# Fixed via haproxy-spoa-hub !165 (platform) + !166 (release).
# v0.7.2 additionally fixed a use-after-free on config reload
# (dangling MetricCtx after a plugin-generation drop, SIGSEGV /
# exit 139 under reload storms) and hardened the fatal-signal
# backtrace handler introduced in v0.7.1.
#
# v0.6.2 skips config reloads when the new file content is byte-
# identical to the last successfully-loaded version. The hub's
# file-watcher fired on every atomic-rename rewrite (HAPTIC's
# deployer rewrites spoa-hub-config.toml on every reconcile
# regardless of content change), tearing down the in-flight plugin
# state on each spurious reload. v0.6.2's content-dedup is still in
# place on v0.7.x — both fixes cooperate: dedup handles the >99%
# of production triggers that are no-op rewrites, drain handles
# the remaining window where a legitimate config change races with
# traffic. Surfaced as the gateway-API conformance
# HTTPRouteRequestMultipleMirrors flake (test polls the mirror
# backend for 10s, the mirror request never lands because the
# spawned task got cancelled when the registry was torn down).
# Fixed via haproxy-spoa-hub !163.
#
# v0.6.1 fixes a use-after-free in MetricRecorder::install() that
# segfaulted the hub on plugin reload. The previous OnceLock-based
# install() was silently no-op on the second-and-later calls, so the
# NEW plugin's hub-provided MetricCtx pointer was never installed;
# the static MetricRecorder in each plugin's .so kept pointing at the
# OLD ctx, and when the hub later dropped the OLD plugin's
# Box<MetricCtx>, the next metric emission anywhere in that .so
# dereferenced freed memory and crashed the hub with SIGSEGV.
# Reproduced in this repo as the HTTPRouteRequestMirror conformance
# flake (intermittent, ~1-in-3 per shard) — fix verified by 2
# consecutive clean conformance runs against the fixed image
# (haproxy-spoa-hub !161, debug branch debug/spoe-log-global on this
# repo). Mandatory bump for anyone running v0.6.0; v0.6.0 is broken
# for any deployment that reloads plugins (i.e. all of them, since
# the haptic controller pushes hub config via the dataplane API).
# v0.6.0 added plugin-API v2 (PLUGIN_API_VERSION 1→2) so plugins can
# emit Prometheus counters / gauges / histograms through the hub's
# existing /metrics endpoint. Vtable evolution is append-only: every
# v1 plugin still loads (the hub gates set_metric_recorder on
# api_version ≥ 2 and silently skips installation for v1 plugins),
# and every v2 plugin still loads against v0.5.x hubs (those hubs
# read only what they know about). Bundled plugins below all bump
# to their v2-consumer releases in the same rollout — see each
# plugin's CHANGELOG for the metric set it now publishes.
# v0.11.0 adds a generic `req_id` SPOE argument the hub adopts as its
# correlation id, so hub log lines carry `span.req_id` matching the JSON
# access log's `req_id` field — previously the hub's identity was a
# synthetic per-frame value nothing outside the hub could join on. The
# chart sends `req_id=unique-id` on every spoe-message
# (libraries/spoa-hub/20-config-content.yaml). Both directions are
# independently compatible: an older hub never looks the argument up, and
# an older chart simply gets hub-generated ids, so this is not a lockstep
# bump. No plugin-API change (still 0.7.1) and no plugin rebuild. New
# metric spoa_request_id_source_total{source}; alert on source="rejected",
# which means the hub replaced a supplied id and its logs no longer match
# the access log. The id is a correlation LABEL — never a cache or map key
# (see the hub's request_id.rs module docs for why).
SPOA_HUB_VERSION="v0.11.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-coraza:coraza registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=coraza
# v0.6.0 makes WAF generations plugin-instance-owned: each reload compiles
# the new policy completely before an atomic swap and retires the old
# generation only after its cached transactions finish, so failed or
# repeated reloads no longer leak WAF handles or disturb the serving
# policy. Response correlation is bounded (max_cached_transactions,
# fail-closed admission), request messages carry an explicit
# body-mode/body-complete contract so truncated bodies never drive WAF
# decisions, unknown application names fail closed, and rule-match
# logging / matched-data exposure are payload-safe opt-ins
# (rule_match_log, matched_data_log, expose_matched_data). The chart's
# generated coraza params emit these keys, so HAPTIC requires >= v0.6.0.
#
# v0.5.0 emits plugin_coraza_evaluations_total / evaluation_duration_seconds /
# denials_total (counters + histogram) via the hub's plugin-API v2 vtable.
# Closes the operational gap where "is the WAF blocking traffic?" needed
# kubectl logs on the haproxy pod — `denials_total{rule_id}` answers the
# question with one PromQL query. Labels: phase ∈ {request, response},
# action is the WAF verdict (allow / deny / detect / ...), app is the
# per-application name from the SPOE arg.
#
# v0.7.0 adds plugin_coraza_rule_hits_total{phase, rule_id, severity, app}:
# one hit per MATCHED rule per evaluation, including on allowed traffic —
# the signal a detect->deny policy rollout needs (denials_total only names
# the single interrupting rule). Severity uses the standard ModSecurity
# names; rules that declare none (CRS reporting rules, -1 from Coraza)
# label as "none". Series product is bounded by matched-rules x apps x
# 2 phases (severity is a dependent label of rule_id). Decode failures of
# the FFI payload surface as rule_hits_decode_errors_total{app}. The
# docs' detect->deny tuning workflow (operations/spoa-hub.md) builds on
# these counters, so HAPTIC pins >= v0.7.0.
SPOA_PLUGIN_CORAZA_VERSION="v0.7.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-external-auth:external-auth registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=external-auth
# v0.5.0 emits plugin_external-auth_auth_requests_total{outcome,status_class}
# + auth_duration_seconds. outcome ∈ {allowed, denied}; status_class is
# the auth-backend HTTP status bucketed to its hundreds digit
# (1xx..5xx + none).
SPOA_PLUGIN_EXTERNAL_AUTH_VERSION="v0.5.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-api-gateway:api-gateway registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=api-gateway
# v0.1.0 adds low-latency JSON request-body validation against configured
# JSON Schemas. Schemas are compiled on plugin init; the request path only
# enforces a bounded body size, validates content-type, parses JSON, and runs
# the in-memory validator. Emits plugin_api_gateway_validation_requests_total.
SPOA_PLUGIN_API_GATEWAY_VERSION="v0.1.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-fingerprinting:fingerprinting registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=fingerprinting
# v0.3.0 emits plugin_fingerprinting_fingerprints_computed_total{kind}
# + compute_duration_seconds{kind}. kind ∈ {ja3, ja4}; JA4 only emits
# when params.ja4 = true.
SPOA_PLUGIN_FINGERPRINTING_VERSION="v0.3.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-maxmind:maxmind registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=maxmind
# v0.4.0 emits plugin_maxmind_lookups_total{lookup,status} + a global
# lookup_duration_seconds histogram. status is LookupStatus::as_str()
# (ok / not_found / error). Histogram is unlabelled by lookup for
# bounded cardinality; per-lookup tails matter less than the global
# p50/p99 for GeoIP sizing.
SPOA_PLUGIN_MAXMIND_VERSION="v0.4.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-mirror:mirror registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=mirror
# HTTP request mirroring plugin, used by the gateway library to back the
# Gateway API HTTPRouteFilter of type RequestMirror (per-route dynamic
# mirror backendRef, optional percentage/fraction sampling).
# v0.5.0 adds per-dispatch SPOE-arg overrides for HTTP-roundtrip
# timeout (arg_timeout_ms) and connection-error retry budget
# (arg_retries) so the gateway library can plumb per-route values
# derived from the primary backend's `timeout server` / `retries`
# (a uniform plugin default is the wrong shape — a mirror should
# wait as long as a normal backend request would). Also raises the
# fallback `timeout_ms` from 200 → 60_000 ms: the previous tight
# default reproduced as `HTTPRouteRequestMultipleMirrors`
# conformance flakes against in-cluster Services that were still
# cold-starting (DNS / kube-proxy propagation race). Fire-and-
# forget dispatch means a long failure tail never affects user-
# visible latency.
# v0.4.0 overrides the v3 plugin-API `drain()` hook to wait for
# in-flight `runtime.spawn`'d HTTP dispatches to complete before
# the hub tears down the plugin runtime on reload. Spin-waits on
# the existing MirrorClient in_flight counter (newly exposed via
# a `pub fn in_flight()` accessor) up to the supplied timeout.
# Closes the HTTPRouteRequestMultipleMirrors cancel-mid-flight
# regression end-to-end: v0.6.2 of the hub mitigated the no-op-
# rewrite trigger (>99% of cases), v0.7.0 + this release close
# the legitimate-config-change-during-traffic window that v0.6.2
# left open. Required hub: v0.7.0+. Older hubs load this plugin
# fine but ignore the appended drain field — same outcome as
# v0.3.0 on those hubs.
# v0.3.0 emits the full metric set: plugin_mirror_dispatches_total /
# dispatch_dropped_total / dispatch_outcomes_total / dispatch_duration_seconds /
# in_flight. Closes the gap where the only signal a mirror was failing
# was kubectl logs on the backend. The saturation rate (dropped /
# dispatches) is the right alert input for tuning request_concurrency.
# v0.2.1: auto-sizes request_concurrency from
# resolved_worker_threads() × 512, clamped to [1024, 4096].
# v0.2.0: adds configurable worker_threads (auto-derived from
# available_parallelism, floored at 2) and pool_max_idle_per_host
# (default 32).
SPOA_PLUGIN_MIRROR_VERSION="v0.6.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-otel:otel registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=otel
# v0.5.0 overrides the v3 plugin-API `drain()` hook to flush
# in-flight OTLP exports (queued in the BatchSpanProcessor /
# PeriodicReader / BatchLogProcessor SDK pipelines) before the
# hub tears down the plugin's tokio runtime on reload. Delegates
# to the plugin's existing `PipelineSet::drain` primitive, which
# was already wired for the in-plugin reload path and calls
# `shutdown()` on each tracer / meter / logger. Limitation:
# the drain body currently doesn't enforce the hub's timeout
# argument itself — falls through to the SDK's own internal
# shutdown timeouts. Today's behaviour is strictly better than
# pre-v0.7 hub (full loss of in-flight exports on reload);
# follow-up to wrap shutdown in tokio::time::timeout can refine
# that. Required hub: v0.7.0+.
# v0.4.0 emits plugin_otel_dispatches_total{outcome} +
# dispatch_duration_seconds. outcome ∈ {processed, uninitialised};
# this covers the SPOE-hop hot path only (the underlying OTLP exporter's
# own retry/drop accounting comes from the opentelemetry-sdk crate).
SPOA_PLUGIN_OTEL_VERSION="v0.5.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-rate-limit:rate-limit registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=rate-limit
# v0.2.0 makes lease (token-bucket) mode's burst real. Previously the
# local lease pool held only rate x sync_interval tokens (4 for
# 1200/min at 200ms) and the configured burst never reached the
# request path, so a browser page load multiplexing ~60 requests
# was mass-429'd while the store bucket sat full (production
# incident 2026-07-18). An empty pool now escalates to one bounded,
# per-key-coalesced store reserve before denying (borrow-on-empty;
# config borrow_on_empty=false restores the old behavior); lease
# refreshes are demand-sized so recurring bursts are pre-stocked
# locally; a borrow that proves the store bucket empty is an honest
# over-limit deny and arms a one-interval backoff so floods cost
# the store at most one round-trip per interval. fail_open is now
# honored when the store is unreachable and the pool is empty
# (previously a drained pool with an open circuit breaker denied
# forever regardless of fail_open — seen during Sentinel
# failovers), and the x-ratelimit-remaining/reset headers report
# the real budget (local pool + last-known store remaining, with a
# replenishment countdown) instead of the tiny lease pool.
# v0.1.4 adds `store_urls` for BYO deployments that intentionally shard across
# several independent Redis/Valkey/Sentinel endpoints, while keeping
# `store_url` as the chart-managed and normal single-store path.
# v0.1.3 warms the shared Valkey connection in the background when store_url is
# configured, preventing exact/GCRA mode from repeatedly paying connection
# setup on the request path and failing closed during normal startup ordering.
# v0.1.2 hardens the shared limiter for DoS-facing use: bounded local key state,
# bounded/coalesced prefetch work, store operation timeouts, lazy Valkey
# reconnect, server-authoritative Valkey time, and immediate active-key rule
# updates. v0.1.1 makes plugin initialization reload-safe by replacing
# process-global OnceLock state with reloadable state. v0.1.0 is the initial
# release: shared, low-latency rate limiting. The default per-request path is
# local (a lease-amortised token bucket enforces the limit in-process); the
# shared store (Valkey) is a background budget allocator in lease mode. Fleet
# overshoot is bounded to N_pods * batch; sustained throughput stays within the
# limit. Opt-in exact mode (GCRA) is available per route for money/contract
# limits.
SPOA_PLUGIN_RATE_LIMIT_VERSION="v0.2.0"

# renovate: datasource=gitlab-packages packageName=haproxy-haptic/haproxy-spoa-hub-plugin-sso-auth:sso-auth registryUrl=https://gitlab.com depName=sso-auth
# v0.3.0 emits plugin_sso_auth_auth_decisions_total{outcome} +
# auth_decision_duration_seconds. outcome maps 1:1 to AuthAction
# variants (redirect / callback / allow / deny / logout / error) so
# operators can chart "redirects accumulating without callbacks"
# (broken SSO flow) in one PromQL query.
SPOA_PLUGIN_SSO_AUTH_VERSION="v0.3.0"

# Glibc version stamped into upstream plugin .so filenames. Tracks the
# upstream build base (Debian bookworm rust:1.94-bookworm builder image).
# Bump in lockstep with upstream when their build base shifts.
SPOA_PLUGIN_GLIBC_VERSION="2.36"
